<div class="summary">
    <p>
        Current work. Rather than scoring a document model’s final answer, I am tracing how its reading of a page
        changes from preprocessing through the vision tower and into the language model, and where it starts to
        diverge from the ground truth.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Why</h2>
<p>
    As models get bigger, understanding why they produced a given output gets harder and more important,
    especially anywhere a wrong answer has consequences. Explainable AI is the umbrella term for the techniques
    that try to open the box: saliency and heatmap attribution that show what a model attended to, probing that
    tests what a layer encodes, and mechanistic interpretability, which tries to reverse-engineer what individual
    components are doing. Very little of that has been aimed at OCR, where the pipeline has an unusually clear
    sequence of stages to trace.
</p>

<h2>What I am doing</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Reviewing the mechanistic interpretability literature to see which techniques transfer to document-understanding models.</li>
    <li>Building heatmap visualizations of a model’s attention as a page moves through preprocessing, the vision tower, and the language model.</li>
    <li>Running a set of experiments to locate where in that pipeline the model’s understanding of a document first diverges from the ground truth.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    It runs alongside the <a href="/research/llm-ocr/">layout-analysis benchmarking</a> and uses the same
    pipeline, so the two projects feed each other.
</p>

<h2>A note on estimating research</h2>
<p>
    The most useful lesson so far has been about planning rather than models. I had a rule of declaring the
    intended output of each work session the night before. I kept being wrong in the same direction: halfway,
    then ninety percent, then several more hours with plenty left, then done, then an entire layer I had been
    ignoring. Meanwhile five days went by with none of the experiments run, because background reading has no
    natural stopping point.
</p>
<p>
    A declared output is an estimate in disguise. To say what you will find, you have to already know the shape
    of the answer, which is exactly the thing that is unknown. Sessions now end by writing down the concrete
    entry point for the next one, which is a record rather than a prediction, and reading days alternate with
    implementation days so open-ended research cannot crowd out the work that produces something runnable.
</p>

